"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,336\nEpisode 6\n\n3\n00:00:07,416 --> 00:00:09,581\nWorking memory limits\n\n4\n00:00:12,381 --> 00:00:14,597\nTry to hold these ten letters in your head.\n\n5\n00:00:14,943 --> 00:00:15,831\nF, B, I, C,\n\n6\n00:00:15,851 --> 00:00:16,738\nI, A, N, A,\n\n7\n00:00:16,758 --> 00:00:17,191\nS, A.\n\n8\n00:00:17,211 --> 00:00:17,860\nHard.\n\n9\n00:00:18,003 --> 00:00:19,072\nNow group them.\n\n10\n00:00:19,092 --> 00:00:19,737\nF B I.\n\n11\n00:00:19,795 --> 00:00:20,705\nC I A.\n\n12\n00:00:20,845 --> 00:00:21,481\nNASA.\n\n13\n00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:23,751\nSame ten letters, three chunks.\n\n14\n00:00:23,843 --> 00:00:25,198\nSuddenly it's easy.\n\n15\n00:00:25,368 --> 00:00:30,155\nWorking memory is the small space where you hold and work on ideas right now.\n\n16\n00:00:30,651 --> 00:00:33,081\nThe classic estimate was seven items,\n\n17\n00:00:33,169 --> 00:00:34,726\ngive or take two.\n\n18\n00:00:35,124 --> 00:00:38,210\nMost researchers now put it closer to four chunks.\n\n19\n00:00:38,469 --> 00:00:42,116\nAnd without rehearsal, what's in it fades within seconds.\n\n20\n00:00:42,286 --> 00:00:44,387\nChunks are where expertise lives.\n\n21\n00:00:44,663 --> 00:00:45,799\nIn a classic study,\n\n22\n00:00:45,819 --> 00:00:49,327\nchess players saw a real game position for five seconds.\n\n23\n00:00:49,692 --> 00:00:52,269\nA master rebuilt twenty or more pieces.\n\n24\n00:00:52,517 --> 00:00:54,032\nA beginner, about four.\n\n25\n00:00:54,467 --> 00:00:56,595\nBut with the pieces scattered at random,\n\n26\n00:00:56,689 --> 00:00:59,034\neveryone managed only three or four.\n\n27\n00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:01,925\nExperts don't have bigger working memories.\n\n28\n00:01:02,197 --> 00:01:03,571\nThey have bigger chunks,\n\n29\n00:01:03,593 --> 00:01:05,744\nstored in long-term memory.\n\n30\n00:01:05,914 --> 00:01:09,206\nSo when a lesson asks for more than those few slots,\n\n31\n00:01:09,411 --> 00:01:11,289\nlearning doesn't just slow down.\n\n32\n00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:12,577\nIt stalls.\n\n33\n00:01:12,974 --> 00:01:16,195\nCognitive load theory splits the demand in two.\n\n34\n00:01:16,538 --> 00:01:19,480\nIntrinsic load comes from the material itself.\n\n35\n00:01:19,834 --> 00:01:22,762\nExtraneous load comes from how it's presented.\n\n36\n00:01:22,932 --> 00:01:25,653\nExtraneous load is the part you control.\n\n37\n00:01:26,116 --> 00:01:28,760\nPut a diagram here and its labels over there,\n\n38\n00:01:28,958 --> 00:01:31,781\nand students must hold one while searching for the other.\n\n39\n00:01:32,233 --> 00:01:33,568\nIntegrating them helps.\n\n40\n00:01:33,941 --> 00:01:38,551\nDecorative images, and on-screen text read aloud word for word,\n\n41\n00:01:38,822 --> 00:01:41,636\nalso take up slots without teaching anything.\n\n42\n00:01:41,806 --> 00:01:42,979\nTwo cautions.\n\n43\n00:01:43,308 --> 00:01:46,184\nCognitive load is hard to measure directly,\n\n44\n00:01:46,342 --> 00:01:48,686\nand the theory has been criticized for that.\n\n45\n00:01:49,165 --> 00:01:53,592\nAnd supports that help beginners can get in the way of more advanced students.\n\n46\n00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:55,556\nAs students gain knowledge,\n\n47\n00:01:55,741 --> 00:01:57,473\ntake the scaffolding down.\n\n48\n00:01:57,643 --> 00:01:58,441\nFor teaching.\n\n49\n00:01:58,843 --> 00:02:01,227\nBreak new material into small steps.\n\n50\n00:02:01,462 --> 00:02:03,004\nPut labels on the diagram,\n\n51\n00:02:03,024 --> 00:02:04,024\nnot beside it.\n\n52\n00:02:04,426 --> 00:02:06,018\nCut what's only decorative.\n\n53\n00:02:06,453 --> 00:02:10,229\nShow worked examples before asking students to solve alone.\n\n54\n00:02:10,598 --> 00:02:15,121\nAnd sequence, so each new idea lands on one they've already chunked.\n\n55\n00:02:15,291 --> 00:02:17,624\nWorking memory is a narrow doorway.\n\n56\n00:02:18,041 --> 00:02:20,836\nGood teaching doesn't push everything through at once.\n\n57\n00:02:21,058 --> 00:02:22,707\nIt builds bigger chunks.\n\n"
